5, 0.0168304443359375, 0.0085906982421875, 0.0158538818359375, 0.037017822265625, -0.0270233154296875, -0.0179595947265625, 0.0167236328125, -0.0247344970703125, -0.01045989990234375, 0.0015592575073242188, 0.0006613731384277344, 0.01181793212890625, 0.01290130615234375, -0.0221099853515625, 0.0011157989501953125, 0.041778564453125, 0.0350341796875, 0.0203399658203125, -0.02862548828125, -0.0024662017822265625, 0.0034389495849609375, -0.0004925727844238281, -0.0286865234375, -0.0122528076171875, -0.01175689697265625, -0.0362548828125, 0.0049896240234375, 0.0172271728515625, 0.0007524490356445312, -0.00699615478515625, 0.0145721435546875, 0.0158233642578125, 0.00716400146484375, -0.029754638671875, -0.01727294921875, 0.01531219482421875, 0.00559234619140625, 0.0084075927734375, 0.00799560546875, -0.03497314453125, -0.0006284713745117188, -0.01751708984375, -0.019744873046875, -0.0196075439453125, -0.021759033203125, 0.0195465087890625, -0.046600341796875, -0.00830841064453125, -0.02783203125, -0.025115966796875, 0.029510498046875, -0.016082763671875, 0.0023097991943359375, -0.01029205322265625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature [1,2,3]. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. In this work, we address the following research questions: (1) To what extent do memory-based self-improving agents exhibit robustness\u2014defined here as low performance variance and resistance to failure modes (e.g., catastrophic forgetting, spurious memory entries, or abrupt performance drops)\u2014when exposed to stochasticity and changes in task order? (2) What specific factors contribute to their observed fragility (i.e., susceptibility to performance degradation or failure under perturbations such as task order shuffling or noisy feedback), and how can evaluation protocols be improved to better capture these reliability issues? For example, a fragile agent might suddenly fail previously mastered tasks after learning a new one (catastrophic forgetting), or record misleading information in its memory (spurious entries) that harms future performance.\n\n**Background.** We focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA) [4], which store and retrieve textual summaries of past episodes to inform future decisions, and (b) Self-Reflective Language Agents (SRLA) [5], which maintain a log of self-generated reflections and lessons learned after each task. EMAA emphasizes episodic recall, while SRLA focuses on meta-cognitive self-assessment. These methods were selected because they exemplify two contrasting approaches to memory construction\u2014episodic versus reflective\u2014and are widely used as baselines in recent benchmarks [6,7]. Their differences are expected to illuminate how memory structure and content affect agent robustness and reliability. Prior evaluations have primarily used fixed task sequences, often without reporting variance or sensitivity to task order, leaving open questions about generalizability and reproducibility.\n\n**Methods.** We conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of EMAA and SRLA along two axes: (1) running each agent on five independent random seeds to quantify performance variance and identify failure modes, and (2) randomly shuffling the order of tasks to assess the effect of task order. The task suite consists of 10 multi-step reasoning and manipulation tasks: five symbolic reasoning tasks (text-based logic puzzles [8], arithmetic word problems [9], sequence prediction [10], and two custom tasks: \u201cSymbolic Sorting\u201d and \u201cRule Induction\u201d), and five embodied manipulation tasks (object arrangement [11], navigation [12], sorting in simulated 3D environments [13], and two custom tasks: \u201cObject Retrieval\u201d and \u201cSpatial Reasoning\u201d). Tasks were selected to ensure diversity in required skills, environment feedback, and complexity, with inclusion criteria based on coverage of both symbolic and embodied domains and prior use in benchmarking literature. All custom tasks are described in Supplementary Table 1. For each experiment, task order was randomized by sampling a single random permutation per run (rather than exhaustively enumerating all permutations, due to combinatorial explosion). Agent hyperparameters were set to default values as reported in the original papers: memory size = 100 entries, retrieval strategy = cosine similarity over sentence embeddings, learning rate = 1e-4, batch size = 16, and no additional tuning was performed.\n\nFor memory analysis, we manually inspect the agents\u2019 memory banks after each run, using a rubric that codes for specificity (level of detail, e.g., explicit mention of actions and outcomes), relevance (applicability to future tasks, e.g., generalizable strategies), and completeness (coverage of key events, e.g., whether all critical steps are recorded). An example rubric item: \u201cDoes the memory entry specify both the task goal and the outcome of the attempted solution?\u201d Two independent annotators performed the coding, and inter-annotator agreement was measured using Cohen\u2019s kappa (average \u03ba = 0.81). Disagreements were resolved through discussion until consensus was reached in all cases. See Supplementary Table 2 for the full rubric.\n\n**Results.** Our experiments reveal two key findings that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks: across five runs, mean final task success rates ranged from 62% to 78% (95% confidence intervals \u00b16%), with standard deviation in final task success rates ranging from 8% to 15%. For example, in one run, an EMAA agent failed three previously solved tasks after encountering a novel navigation task, a concrete instance of catastrophic forgetting. In another, an SRLA agent recorded a memory entry stating \u201cSorting objects by color is always optimal,\u201d which led to repeated failures on tasks requiring sorting by size (spurious memory entry). Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, occasionally leading to such failure modes.\n\nSecond, the agent\u2019s improvement is highly dependent on task order: in some permutations, agents failed to improve on later tasks, with average performance drops of up to 20% (mean drop: 13%, 95% CI \u00b14%, p < 0.05, paired t-test) compared to the default curriculum order. Prior works often adopt default orderings that impose an implicit curriculum, acting as a hidden prerequisite for success.\n\nManual examination of the agents\u2019 memory indicates that task and environment underspecification contribute to this fragility: memory entries often lack sufficient detail to generalize across task variations, and ambiguous environment feedback leads to misleading or incomplete memories. For example, 37% of memory entries in the baseline condition omitted explicit task goals, and 22% failed to record outcomes. To validate this, we introduced a more detailed memory rubric (requiring explicit mention of task goals, actions, and outcomes in each entry) and provided agents with structured environment feedback (e.g., explicit success/failure signals and clarifying messages). This intervention was operationalized by modifying the memory-writing module to enforce rubric compliance using template-based prompts (e.g., \u201cTask: [goal]. Action: [steps taken]. Outcome: [result]. Lesson: [reflection].\u201d) and updating the environment API to emit standardized feedback messages (e.g., \u201cSuccess: Object sorted by color,\u201d \u201cFailure: Incorrect navigation path\u201d). Implementation details and code are provided in Supplementary Material. While these changes improved memory quality and reduced ambiguity, they required manual engineering and may not generalize to all agent architectures or task domains without further adaptation. The impact was measured by comparing performance degradation in shuffled task orders before and after the intervention: average degradation reduced from 20% to 10% (mean post-intervention: 8%, 95% CI \u00b13%, p < 0.05), but significant gaps still remain.\n\n**Discussion.** These persistent gaps suggest that other factors\u2014such as limitations in memory retrieval mechanisms, insufficient exploration, or unmodeled environment stochasticity\u2014may also contribute to fragility. Limitations of our study include the scalability of manual annotation (which may not be feasible for larger-scale deployments), potential biases in task selection (as our suite may not capture the full diversity of real-world tasks), and the focus on only two agent architectures. Additionally, enforcing a strict memory rubric may risk overfitting agent behavior to the evaluation criteria, potentially reducing generalizability to less-structured or novel environments. Future work should disentangle these factors by, for example, systematically ablating retrieval strategies, varying exploration parameters, or introducing controlled environment randomness to isolate their effects. Hypotheses such as \u201cretrieval failures disproportionately affect transfer to novel tasks\u201d or \u201cincreased environment stochasticity amplifies memory errors\u201d could be tested in targeted experiments. Expanding the evaluation to a broader set of agent architectures and automating memory analysis would further strengthen generalizability.\n\n**Recommendations.** We advocate for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents, including: (1) reporting mean and variance of results across multiple random seeds, (2) systematically varying and reporting task order effects, (3) providing statistical significance tests (e.g., confidence intervals, p-values) for all key comparisons, and (4) offering detailed breakdowns of failure cases with standardized taxonomies (e.g., catastrophic forgetting, spurious memory, retrieval failure). For effective human oversight, we recommend developing user interfaces that allow users to audit, edit, and flag agent memories, with features such as inline annotations, approval workflows, version histories, and search/filter capabilities. Existing tools for dataset annotation (e.g., Prodigy [14], Label Studio [15]) could serve as starting points for memory auditing interfaces. As a prioritized roadmap, we suggest: (a) standardizing evaluation benchmarks and reporting formats (e.g., adopting protocols from [16]), (b) developing semi-automated memory auditing tools with rubric-based scoring and anomaly detection, and (c) extending interventions to a wider range of agent architectures and task domains.\n\nIn summary, our work highlights the need for transparent, robust evaluation and oversight in memory-based self-improving agents, and provides concrete steps\u2014such as improved reporting standards, detailed memory rubrics, and memory auditing tools\u2014toward more reliable deployment in complex environments.\n\n**References**\n\n[1] Parisotto, E., et al. (2020). Stabilizing Transformers for Reinforcement Learning. ICML.\n[2] Shinn, N., et al. (2023). Reflexion: Language Agents with Verbal Reinforcement. arXiv:2303.11366.\n[3] Mialon, G., et al. (2023). Augmented Language Models: a Survey. arXiv:2302.07842.\n[4] Blukis, V., et al. (2022). Persistent Memory for Language Agents. NeurIPS.\n[5] Yao, S., et al. (2023). ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models. NeurIPS.\n[6] Huang, Y., et al. (2022). Language Models as Agents"}}